     "title": "How Scientific Consensus Really Forms",
     "content": "The Bohr-Einstein story is a case study with a lesson beyond quantum mechanics itself: it shows that scientific consensus is a social process layered on top of an empirical one, not a pure referendum on evidence arriving the instant enough data exists. After Bohr's circle won the reputational battles of the 1920s and 1930s, Copenhagen-style thinking was written into the textbooks that trained the next generation of physicists, most influentially through Bohr's own Copenhagen institute, which hosted and shaped many of the physicists who went on to hold professorships elsewhere. Cold War-era physics education, driven by urgent practical demands (nuclear technology, then the space race, then solid-state electronics), further entrenched a culture sometimes summarized as 'shut up and calculate': students were trained to use quantum mechanics's mathematical machinery fluently and were actively discouraged from spending time on foundational questions about what it all meant, since interpretation was treated as a solved, closed matter rather than an open one. This pedagogical culture is itself a historical choice, not a logical necessity. Meanwhile, an alternative interpretation, David Bohm's 1952 pilot-wave theory (built on ideas Louis de Broglie had proposed decades earlier), reproduced all the same experimental predictions as Copenhagen quantum mechanics while restoring a picture in which particles have definite trajectories at all times, guided by a real physical wave. It was not refuted by any experiment; it was largely ignored, partly because Einstein and others who might have championed it lacked Bohr's institutional reach, and partly because the physics community had already settled into the Copenhagen consensus by the time Bohm published. Only after John Bell's 1964 theorem reopened foundational questions with new experimental teeth did serious interest in alternatives revive. The broader takeaway: understanding why a scientific idea became dominant requires looking at evidence and institutions, prestige, and pedagogy together, a lens historians and sociologists of science apply not just to quantum mechanics but to how any field settles its foundational questions.",
